WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.708
Picture a theatre's prompt book.

2
00:00:04.708 --> 00:00:06.401
It follows a strict layout.

3
00:00:06.401 --> 00:00:11.483
The first page carries a stamp, naming the edition of the house rules it follows.

4
00:00:11.483 --> 00:00:15.812
Then comes the glossary, a numbered list of every name the book uses.

5
00:00:15.812 --> 00:00:16.941
And then the cues.

6
00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:20.776
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:20.776 --> 00:00:22.484
A class file is just bytes.

8
00:00:22.484 --> 00:00:26.658
So how does anything know where one part ends and the next begins?

9
00:00:26.658 --> 00:00:30.011
And why would anyone need a whole library to read it?

10
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.106
In lesson 2, javap showed us a class as a tidy listing.

11
00:00:35.106 --> 00:00:43.337
Underneath, a class file is an array of bytes with a fixed layout, set out in chapter 4 of the JVM specification.

12
00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:49.232
Here are the first ten bytes of java/lang/Object.class, on JDK 25.

13
00:00:49.232 --> 00:00:55.928
The first four are the magic number, CAFEBABE: the stamp that says, this is a class file.

14
00:00:56.133 --> 00:00:59.770
Every class file you have ever run carries those same four bytes.

15
00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.568
It must be the most printed stamp in the building.

16
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.272
Next come two version numbers, minor and major, two bytes each.

17
00:01:08.272 --> 00:01:09.455
A small puzzle.

18
00:01:09.455 --> 00:01:11.823
The major version reads 00 45.

19
00:01:11.823 --> 00:01:13.953
Which Java wrote this page?

20
00:01:13.953 --> 00:01:15.216
Pause and guess.

21
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.591
Hex 45 is 69.

22
00:01:19.591 --> 00:01:26.332
From Java 5 on, the Java version is the major version minus 44.

23
00:01:26.332 --> 00:01:28.151
So 69 is Java 25.

24
00:01:28.151 --> 00:01:33.608
The labs' own classes say 41, which is 65: Java 21.

25
00:01:33.833 --> 00:01:35.343
And the stamp matters.

26
00:01:35.343 --> 00:01:42.479
A JVM refuses a class file whose major version is newer than its own, with UnsupportedClassVersionError.

27
00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:47.763
An old stage manager won't run a book printed under rules they've never read.

28
00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:55.614
Every number in the file is big-endian, biggest byte first, which is exactly how DataInputStream reads.

29
00:01:55.614 --> 00:02:04.787
So the first four fields take four lines: readInt for the magic number, then three unsigned shorts, for minor, major and the pool count.

30
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.296
Then comes the glossary.

31
00:02:07.296 --> 00:02:13.612
Its real name is the constant pool: a numbered table of every name and constant the class uses.

32
00:02:13.612 --> 00:02:15.274
And here's the hard part.

33
00:02:15.274 --> 00:02:20.660
Each entry starts with a one-byte tag, and the tag decides how many bytes follow.

34
00:02:20.867 --> 00:02:25.510
A Utf8 entry, a piece of text, has a two-byte length, then the text.

35
00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:31.383
A Class entry has two bytes: the number of the Utf8 entry that holds the class's name.

36
00:02:31.383 --> 00:02:34.593
A Methodref, a reference to a method, has four.

37
00:02:34.593 --> 00:02:37.597
And a Long or a Double takes up two numbers.

38
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.762
Here the prompt book picture breaks.

39
00:02:40.762 --> 00:02:45.098
A printed glossary has line breaks, so your eye finds the next entry.

40
00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:46.355
The bytes have none.

41
00:02:46.355 --> 00:02:50.943
To find the tenth entry, you read the first nine, and add up their sizes.

42
00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:55.580
Remember lesson 2's javap listing of StackDemo.

43
00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.950
Entry 7 is a Long, a thousand.

44
00:02:57.950 --> 00:02:59.925
The next line is entry 9.

45
00:02:59.925 --> 00:03:01.111
Where did 8 go?

46
00:03:01.111 --> 00:03:02.375
Pause and guess.

47
00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:05.922
Nowhere.

48
00:03:05.922 --> 00:03:12.996
The eight-byte constant at 7 sits in two seats, like a cellist who buys a second ticket for the cello.

49
00:03:12.996 --> 00:03:18.059
Seat 7 is the cellist, seat 8 is the cello, and the next guest sits in 9.

50
00:03:18.267 --> 00:03:21.095
Now say your reader counts every entry once.

51
00:03:21.095 --> 00:03:22.509
The Long at 7 is fine.

52
00:03:22.509 --> 00:03:26.109
But it calls the next entry 8, when the file calls it 9.

53
00:03:26.109 --> 00:03:34.080
Every number after that is off by one, and the walk runs one entry past the end of the glossary, into the bytes that follow.

54
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.241
So here's the rule.

55
00:03:36.241 --> 00:03:41.725
Each entry's size depends on its tag, so one wrong count shifts everything after it.

56
00:03:41.725 --> 00:03:43.553
Count Long and Double twice.

57
00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:50.643
After the glossary come the access flags, then this_class, the number of the Class entry that names this class.

58
00:03:50.643 --> 00:03:55.599
Then the superclass, the interfaces, the fields, the methods and the attributes.

59
00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.924
Now picture doing this for real.

60
00:03:58.924 --> 00:04:03.772
The house rules change with every JDK release, every six months.

61
00:04:03.772 --> 00:04:07.787
Until JDK 24, the JDK had no public library for this.

62
00:04:07.787 --> 00:04:15.059
So frameworks borrowed a printing press from outside: ASM, the long-standing class-file library.

63
00:04:15.059 --> 00:04:17.483
Byte Buddy carries its own copy.

64
00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:23.142
Twice a year the rules change, and the whole street lines up at the press shop for new type.

65
00:04:23.142 --> 00:04:25.863
So the real cost isn't reading the bytes once.

66
00:04:25.863 --> 00:04:27.341
It's keeping up, forever.

67
00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:36.150
Since JDK 24, Java has a press of its own: the ClassFile API, in the package java.lang.classfile.

68
00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:39.645
On JDK 25 you use it with no special flags.

69
00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:46.228
It reads a class, prints new ones, and copies a class while changing parts of it.

70
00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:48.011
So, the names.

71
00:04:48.011 --> 00:04:52.021
The stamp is the magic number, then the minor and major version.

72
00:04:52.021 --> 00:04:54.152
The glossary is the constant pool.

73
00:04:54.152 --> 00:04:56.909
And the cues are the methods and their code.

74
00:04:57.833 --> 00:04:58.913
Rules of thumb.

75
00:04:58.913 --> 00:05:01.936
Walk the format by hand once, to learn it.

76
00:05:01.936 --> 00:05:05.031
For every class, on every JDK, use a press.

77
00:05:05.031 --> 00:05:13.092
And on UnsupportedClassVersionError, read the stamp: the file was printed under newer rules than that JVM knows.

78
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.013
So, how does anything know where one part ends?

79
00:05:17.013 --> 00:05:21.244
By counting from the stamp, with every tag deciding the next step.

80
00:05:21.244 --> 00:05:25.988
One slip breaks everything after it, so the JDK now carries its own press.

81
00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:27.949
Quick check.

82
00:05:27.949 --> 00:05:33.108
The labs' own classes start with ca fe ba be, 00 00, 00 41.

83
00:05:33.108 --> 00:05:35.731
Which Java version wrote them?

84
00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:42.727
Hex 41 is 65, and 65 minus 44 is 21.

85
00:05:42.727 --> 00:05:47.404
Java 21, the release the build compiles for.
